diff --git a/components/thoron_portable_key/README.md b/components/thoron_portable_key/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..4aec38d136c65e476ddc18cbf4f94a9f7d632797
--- /dev/null
+++ b/components/thoron_portable_key/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,44 @@
+# Thoron portable key
+
+The passphrase of a portable copy of Thoron (ROADMAP P4.9; design 7.8; threat model section 6 of
+Thoron's repository). This directory holds the part that needs no window: the key file, its locks
+and how it is written. Hooking it into `os_crypt` and the unlock window are separate steps.
+
+## What it keeps
+
+A portable copy keeps its saved passwords and cookies encrypted as Chromium does (`os_crypt`,
+AES-256-GCM, values that start with `v10`) with one random 32-byte key, the profile key. An
+installed copy keeps that key in `Local State`, protected by Windows (DPAPI) for one account on one
+PC. A portable copy locked with a passphrase keeps it in `Thoron Portable Key` in its User Data
+folder instead, sealed several times:
+
+| Lock | Opens with | Sealing key |
+|---|---|---|
+| passphrase (always, once) | the passphrase | Argon2id (RFC 9106) of the passphrase with the salt and the cost the lock names; at least 64 MiB, 3 passes, 4 lanes |
+| recovery (optional, once) | the recovery key on paper | HKDF-SHA256 of the recovery key's 128 random bits with the lock's salt |
+| remembered PC (0 to 6) | this PC, this Windows account | DPAPI, nothing more is needed on that PC |
+
+Each seal is AES-256-GCM over the profile key and binds what the lock says about itself (its
+kind, cost, salt). The whole file carries an HMAC-SHA256 made with a key derived from the
+profile key, so once any lock opens, a file changed in any other way is refused. The format is
+described byte by byte in `key_file.h`; nothing else is read.
+
+## Files
+
+- `argon2id.h`: Argon2id from `//third_party/argon2` (the reference code of its authors), the cost
+  limits (floor and ceiling) and the calibration on the PC that sets up the passphrase.
+- `recovery_key.h`: the recovery key, 128 random bits and a 32-bit check, written as eight groups
+  of four characters of Crockford's base 32.
+- `key_file.h`: the file's format, reading it, the locks, opening it.
+- `key_store.h`: writing the file so that a pulled drive never leaves a copy that cannot be
+  opened, and finishing an interrupted write when it is read.
+- `os_key_store.h`: the system's store for "Remember this PC" (DPAPI on Windows).
+- `secret_bytes.h`: bytes that are wiped when they go.
+
+## Tests
+
+`thoron_portable_key_unittests` (`group("thoron_portable_key_all")`): the RFC 9106 Argon2id test
+vector, the round trips (passphrase, recovery key, a remembered PC, a "new PC" with a fresh
+store), every changed field of the file, locks added or removed, the cost floor, the format's
+strictness, the passphrase's normal form, the pulled drive after every step of a write, and DPAPI.
+`thoron_portable_key_file_fuzzer` fuzzes the reader (built only with `use_libfuzzer`).
